00:57:08,400 --> 00:57:17,120
the best of ourselves to the world. Seneca put it clearly: "It is not that we have little time,

491
00:57:17,120 --> 00:57:24,040
but that we lose a lot." This reminds us that we must make our time an

492
00:57:24,040 --> 00:57:31,040
investment in our well-being, instead of dispersing it on activities that do not serve us.

493
00:57:31,040 --> 00:57:36,360
The importance of self-care is related to our ability to

494
00:57:36,360 --> 00:57:43,960
be strong in times of adversity. When we ignore our inner needs,

495
00:57:43,960 --> 00:57:50,520
our mind weakens, and consequently, our ability to face

496
00:57:50,520 --> 00:57:58,360
life's challenges decreases. Lack of rest, poor nutrition or lack of time for

497
00:57:58,360 --> 00:58:04,320
reflection can lead us to exhaustion, making us more susceptible to falling into

498
00:58:04,320 --> 00:58:13,240
despair or impulsive reactions to obstacles. Thus, self-care becomes

499
00:58:13,240 --> 00:58:19,160
a form of prevention; It is a way of training the mind so that, when

500
00:58:19,160 --> 00:58:24,240
the storm comes, we are sufficiently prepared to stand firm.

501
00:58:24,240 --> 00:58:32,160
Epictetus teaches us that we must be aware of our limitations and learn to respect them:

502
00:58:32,160 --> 00:58:38,600
"Do not harm yourself by doing what you should not do." This Stoic approach

503
00:58:38,600 --> 00:58:45,840
highlights the importance of not exhausting ourselves in the pursuit of achievement or pleasing others,

