01:13:03,160 --> 01:13:09,600
Trust in the process is key to this transformation, as it allows you to continue,

629
01:13:09,600 --> 01:13:17,920
even when the results are not yet visible. Additionally, trusting the process helps us

630
01:13:17,920 --> 01:13:25,240
have a growth mindset. When we face difficulties or obstacles, it is easy

631
01:13:25,240 --> 01:13:31,280
to be tempted to think that we are failing. However, if we trust that

632
01:13:31,280 --> 01:13:38,240
we are exactly where we need to be and that the lessons we learn now are critical

633
01:13:38,240 --> 01:13:46,200
for our future, we can see every challenge as a learning opportunity. Heraclitus,

634
01:13:46,200 --> 01:13:54,320
another great thinker of ancient philosophy, said that “change is the only constant in life.”

635
01:13:54,320 --> 01:14:01,040
When we trust the process, we understand that difficulties are not definitive,

636
01:14:01,040 --> 01:14:08,240
but rather natural parts of our path. Therefore, trusting the process is a practice

637
01:14:08,240 --> 01:14:16,800
that allows us to live with more serenity, clarity and balance. By letting go of the need

638
01:14:16,800 --> 01:14:23,680
to control every detail of life, we leave room for growth, transformation and

639
01:14:23,680 --> 01:14:30,960
evolution. Trusting the process teaches us that time, more than an enemy, is our ally,

640
01:14:30,960 --> 01:14:37,800
and that true freedom comes when we accept that we are part of something bigger, which will always

641
01:14:37,800 --> 01:14:44,840
be guiding us towards our destiny. This trust strengthens us, gives us courage

642
01:14:44,840 --> 01:14:51,920
to move forward and reminds us that, although we cannot control every aspect of life,

643
01:14:51,920 --> 01:14:58,600
we can always trust that the path we are traveling is the right one.

644
01:14:58,600 --> 01:15:04,120
13. The Power of Patience. Patience is often seen as

645
01:15:04,120 --> 01:15:12,240
a passive virtue, something that only involves waiting without acting. However, in Stoic philosophy,

646
01:15:12,240 --> 01:15:19,520
patience is considered an active and transformative force. It is not just about waiting, but

647
01:15:19,520 --> 01:15:27,320
about finding strength in that waiting process, maintaining serenity in the face of adversity and

648
01:15:27,320 --> 01:15:34,800
being able to perceive the lessons that time offers us. As Seneca said: “Most

649
01:15:34,800 --> 01:15:42,320
things cannot be controlled, but they can be accepted with patience.” This acceptance of

650
01:15:42,320 --> 01:15:49,440
what we cannot control is the first step in developing a truly invincible mind.

651
01:15:49,440 --> 01:15:55,200
In a world where the rush is constant, patience has become a

652
01:15:55,200 --> 01:16:02,200
rare and valuable skill. We are used to wanting immediate results, to looking for

653
01:16:02,200 --> 01:16:10,160
quick solutions to our problems and not knowing how to wait for the right moment for things to happen. Patience

654
01:16:10,160 --> 01:16:18,400
, however, teaches us that time is an ally, not an enemy. Epictetus also

655
01:16:18,400 --> 01:16:25,480
reminds us that “it is not what happens to you, but how you react to it that matters.” That is,

656
01:16:25,480 --> 01:16:31,920
by cultivating patience, we train our minds to respond calmly and clearly

657
01:16:31,920 --> 01:16:37,440
to life's situations, instead of being dominated by anxiety and impatience.

658
01:16:37,440 --> 01:16:44,040
The power of patience lies in its ability to transform moments

659
01:16:44,040 --> 01:16:51,000
difficult growth opportunities. When we face difficulties, our immediate impulse

660
01:16:51,000 --> 01:16:59,680
is often to try to escape the situation as quickly as possible. However,

661
01:16:59,680 --> 01:17:06,720
if we are patient, we can reflect on what is happening, learn from experiences

662
01:17:06,720 --> 01:17:13,840
, and understand that every challenge is an opportunity to strengthen our character and our mind.

663
01:17:13,840 --> 01:17:21,280
Patience is not about waiting without doing anything; It is about maintaining constant action,

664
01:17:21,280 --> 01:17:27,600
but with the understanding that the results do not have to come immediately. As Marcus Aurelius said

665
01:17:27,600 --> 01:17:34,960
: “What is in the way advances the path. “What is in the way, becomes

666
01:17:34,960 --> 01:17:40,960
the way.” That is, what initially seems like an obstacle can be revealed as

667
01:17:40,960 --> 01:17:47,720
the path to our development, if we have the patience to wait for the right moment.

668
01:17:47,720 --> 01:17:54,840
In the Stoic context, patience does not mean indifference. It does not mean resignation

669
01:17:55,440 --> 01:18:02,880
or passivity in the face of difficulties. On the contrary, it is an attitude of active acceptance: recognizing that

670
01:18:02,880 --> 01:18:09,800
things happen at their own pace and that, to achieve true understanding and wisdom,

671
01:18:09,800 --> 01:18:17,520
we must go through certain processes and cycles. By cultivating patience, we develop a

672
01:18:17,520 --> 01:18:24,000
long-term view, a perspective that every experience, good or bad,

673
01:18:24,000 --> 01:18:31,000
has intrinsic value and can teach us something critical to our journey.

674
01:18:31,000 --> 01:18:41,308
A clear example of this is found in the field of self-growth. Throughout our lives,

675
01:18:41,308 --> 01:18:45,240
we seek to improve our health, our relationships, and our careers. Often, we see

676
01:18:45,240 --> 01:18:52,520
progress at certain times, but at others, the path seems to stagnate. Patience allows us

677
01:18:52,520 --> 01:18:58,760
to understand that true growth takes time and that every small action we take,

678
01:18:58,760 --> 01:19:05,160
